  "query_preview": "..You heard it here first folks. Sunday looks like a \npretty nice day across much of the Panhandle and coast. Expect \nwinds to start dying off Friday as our parade of lows comes to an \nend, allowing high pressure to once again briefly dominate, \nincreasing the odds for a little sun, primarily Sunday. The \nbiggest question in the extended forecast is a shortwave trough \nmoving in from the western Gulf Saturday, which has a decent \nsignal of colder air associated with it. 850mb temperatures near \n-5C certainly make one ponder the rain/snow line for Icy Strait; \nhowever, the long transit over relatively warm ocean waters will \nhelp warm the surface airmass, along with onshore southerly flow \nscrubbing out colder air at the surface, likely limiting sea level\nimpacts to rain. Something to track through the rest of this week\nif this system starts trending colder, which could mean a hit of \nsnow for Icy Strait north. Either way on Sunday, mariners looking \nto make moves across the inner channels should enjoy lighter \nwinds, keeping a close eye on major inlets like Taiya, Taku, and \nStikine for breezy northerly to easterly winds. This may help \nprime some northern communities for additional snow later on next \nweek.\n\nFor folks operating along the mighty Gulf coast, fresh seas will \nwind down Friday with long period southerly swell flourishing, \nbecoming westerly by the end of the weekend. By and large \nsignificant wave heights should reach near or below 5 to 7 ft by \nSunday, existing within a more comfortable long period swell \nspectrum. The exception may be waters west of Prince of Wales \nwhere we could see 7 to 9 ft fresh seas from higher northwesterly \nwinds. Monday, the Aleutian Storm Track opens up opportunities for\nstorm development into the Gulf into next week. \n\nHigh confidence for lighter winds in the inner channels and the \noverall marine forecast thru Sunday. Keep an eye for fog in the \nforecast as we move closer to the weekend. The largest uncertainty\nin the long range is cold air advection on the backside of the \nshortwave trough Saturday which has implications on precipitation \ntype for the north. As of Tuesday night, the published forecast \nreflects high confidence for rain Saturday with room to pivot if \nthe system trends colder.",
